                  "labelZh": "解釋",
                  "sourceLabel": "Explanation",
                  "textEn": "The clearest examples would be things like job interviews, exams, flights, medical appointments, or important official meetings. In those situations, cutting it fine is usually a bad idea, because one small delay can throw everything off. Traffic, long queues, security checks, or even getting lost can all happen when you least need them. So, arriving early gives people a bit of breathing room. It is basically a buffer against chaos, and sometimes that extra fifteen or twenty minutes can save the day.",
                  "textZh": "最明顯的例子包括求職面試、考試、航班、醫療預約，或者重要的正式會議。在這些情況下，把時間算得太緊通常都不是好主意，因為只要出現一點小延誤，整件事就可能被打亂。交通擠塞、排長龍、安檢程序，甚至迷路，往往都會在最不適合的時候出現。所以，提早到達能給人留下一點喘息空間。這基本上就像替混亂預留一個緩衝位，而有時那多出來的十五或二十分鐘，真的可以救整個局面。",
