                  "labelZh": "解釋",
                  "sourceLabel": "Explanation",
                  "textEn": "Every culture has its own unwritten rules about what counts as polite behaviour at the table. In some places, speaking loudly, helping yourself freely, or eating quickly may feel natural and friendly, whereas in other places the same behaviour may seem rude or poorly mannered. In that sense, table manners are a bit like a social language: people may not always explain them out loud, but they still send clear signals about consideration, self-control, and how much respect you show to others.",
                  "textZh": "每一種文化，其實都有自己一套關於甚麼才算餐桌禮貌的「不成文規矩」。在某些地方，大聲說話、自己隨意夾菜，或者吃得比較快，可能會令人覺得自然又親切；但在另一些地方，同樣的行為卻可能被視為無禮，甚至欠缺教養。從這個角度來看，餐桌禮儀有點像一種社交語言：人們未必總是把它明說出來，但它仍然會清楚傳遞出一個人是否體貼、是否懂得克制，以及是否尊重他人。",
