                  "textEn": "Table manners do not appear out of thin air; they usually grow out of real daily life. If a culture traditionally shares dishes in the middle of the table, people may develop rules about serving others, using chopsticks properly, or not digging around for the best piece. If people usually eat individual portions, the manners may lean more towards personal boundaries and formal table settings. In other words, manners are not random decorations around a meal; they are shaped by the meal itself, along with the history behind it.",
