                  "labelZh": "解釋",
                  "sourceLabel": "Explanation",
                  "textEn": "The main reason is that housing is often expensive, space is limited, and competition can be quite fierce. In other words, people are usually trying to juggle three things at once: price, location, and size — and more often than not, something has to give. If a flat is cheap, it may be tiny or far away; if it is in a convenient area, the rent may be eye-watering. So, for many people, finding a place to live can feel a bit like trying to squeeze into a coat that was never quite made for you.",
                  "textZh": "主要原因是，住屋通常很貴、空間有限，而且競爭也相當激烈。換句話說，人們往往都在同時平衡三件事：價錢、地點和面積——而很多時候，總有一樣要作出犧牲。如果一個單位比較便宜，它可能會很小，或者位置很遠；如果它位於方便的地區，租金往往又高得令人咋舌。所以，對很多人來說，找地方住有時真的像硬要把自己塞進一件本來就不太合身的外套一樣。",
