                  "sourceLabel": "Explanation",
                  "textEn": "The main reason is that events in other countries often spill across borders. A war, an economic crisis, a disease outbreak, or even a major election elsewhere can affect prices, travel, jobs, public opinion, and daily life in ways people may not expect. In that sense, foreign news is not really “someone else’s business” anymore. The world is smaller than it looks on the map. If people never look beyond their own doorstep, their understanding of reality can become a little too narrow.",
                  "textZh": "主要原因是，其他國家發生的事情很多時候都會跨越邊界，影響到我們。戰爭、經濟危機、疾病爆發，甚至外地的重要選舉，都可能以人們意想不到的方式影響物價、旅遊、工作、輿論，以及日常生活。從這個角度來說，外國新聞其實已經不再只是「別人的事」。世界比地圖上看起來要小得多。如果人們從來不把目光放到自己門外，他們對現實的理解就很容易變得太狹窄。",
